#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10773
msgid "`cir.sub` (::cir::SubOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10775
msgid "_Integer subtraction_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10783
msgid ""
"The `cir.sub` operation performs subtraction on integer operands. Both "
"operands and the result must have the same integer, vector-of-integer or "
"matrix-of-integer type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10787
msgid ""
"The optional `nuw` (no unsigned wrap) and `nsw` (no signed wrap) flags "
"indicate that the result is poison if unsigned or signed overflow occurs, "
"respectively; both may be given as `nuw nsw`. Saturating subtraction is "
"`cir.sub.sat`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10823
msgid "`cir.sub.overflow` (::cir::SubOverflowOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10825
msgid "_Integer subtraction with overflow checking_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10834
msgid ""
"`cir.sub.overflow` performs subtraction with overflow checking on integral "
"operands. See `CIR_BinOpOverflow` for semantics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10865
msgid "`cir.sub.sat` (::cir::SubSatOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10867
msgid "_Saturating integer subtraction_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10875
msgid ""
"The `cir.sub.sat` operation subtracts its integer operands and clamps the "
"result to the range of the element type instead of wrapping. The clamp is "
"signed or unsigned according to the element type; vectors and matrices "
"saturate elementwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10908
msgid "`cir.switch` (::cir::SwitchOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10910
msgid "_Switch operation_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10921
msgid ""
"The `cir.switch` operation represents C/C++ switch functionality for "
"conditionally executing multiple regions of code. The operand to an switch "
"is an integral condition value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10925
msgid ""
"Besides taking an integer condition and CIR regions, it also accepts an "
"`all_enum_cases_covered` attribute indicating whether all enum cases are  "
"handled by the operation. Note that the presence of a default CaseOp does  "
"not imply `all_enum_cases_covered`. The original AST switch must explicitly "
"list  every enum case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10931
msgid ""
"The set of `cir.case` operations and their enclosing `cir.switch` represent "
"the semantics of a C/C++ switch statement. Users can use "
"`collectCases(llvm::SmallVector<CaseOp> &cases)` to collect the `cir.case` "
"operation in the `cir.switch` operation easily."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10936
msgid ""
"The `cir.case` operations don't have to be in the region of `cir.switch` "
"directly. However, when all the `cir.case` operations live in the region of "
"`cir.switch` directly and there are no other operations except the ending "
"`cir.yield` operation in the region of `cir.switch` directly, we say the "
"`cir.switch` operation is in a simple form. Users can use `bool "
"isSimpleForm(llvm::SmallVector<CaseOp> &cases)` member function to detect if "
"the `cir.switch` operation is in a simple form. The simple form makes it "
"easier for analyses to handle the `cir.switch` operation and makes the "
"boundary to give up clear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:10946
msgid ""
"To make the simple form as common as possible, CIR code generation attaches "
"operations corresponding to the statements that lives between top level "
"cases into the closest `cir.case` operation."
